Because metacognition is one of the clearest things consciousness can do, so people mistake a powerful function of consciousness for consciousness itself.

Metacognition is roughly:

cognition about cognition — monitoring, modeling, evaluating, or controlling your own mental processes.

Examples: “I’m uncertain,” “I know that I know this,” “I’m getting angry,” “my attention just wandered,” “that memory might be wrong.”

Consciousness is the broader phenomenon: there being something it is like for the system to be in a state at all—seeing red, feeling pain, hearing music, experiencing a thought, having a sense of presence.

The confusion comes from several places.

First, metacognition makes consciousness legible. A creature can have an experience, but once it can say or represent, “I am having this experience,” researchers suddenly have something measurable. So confidence judgments, introspective reports, error awareness, and self-reports become experimental proxies for consciousness. Eventually the proxy starts getting treated as the thing itself.

Second, metacognition creates the recursive structure that feels especially consciousness-like:

world → perception → thought → thought-about-thought → "me thinking"

That final recursive layer creates a particularly strong sense of a self standing behind experience. It is easy to conclude that this observer-of-the-observer is where consciousness begins.

But it probably isn’t that simple.

You can plausibly have:

Consciousness without much metacognition. A baby feeling warmth, an animal experiencing pain, being absorbed in music, or seeing something before explicitly reflecting on seeing it.

And you can have:

Metacognitive computation without full conscious awareness. The brain can monitor uncertainty, detect errors, alter strategies, or estimate confidence without all of those computations themselves becoming phenomenally experienced.

So I would separate at least three layers:

Cognition: information processing.
Consciousness: experienced/accessed mental state.
Metacognition: a system constructing information about its own cognition and potentially regulating it.

And then there is an especially interesting fourth thing:

Meta-awareness — becoming conscious of something that was already occurring in consciousness.

For example, you can be anxious for ten minutes and then suddenly notice:

“Oh. I’m anxious.”

The anxiety did not begin when the metacognition appeared. What changed was that the system re-represented its own state.

That distinction matters enormously. It suggests that the apparent little person inside your head observing everything—the Cartesian spectator—isn’t necessarily consciousness itself. It may be a model consciousness constructs of its own activity.

Which connects directly to your earlier point about the face: the face is an externally addressable representation of the person; similarly, the introspective “I” may be an internally addressable representation of the experiencing system.

The handle isn’t necessarily the thing being handled.